INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1658,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/spain-startups-barcelona-supercomputing-center/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/spain-startups-barcelona-supercomputing-center','Spain-Based Startups Advance Regional Innovation With Barcelona Supercomputing Center','spainbasedstartupsadvanceregionalinnovationwithbarcelonasupercomputingcenter','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-20T05:00:00Z','2026-10-21T03:01:33Z','The Barcelona Supercomputing Center (BSC)’s AI Factory Accelerator Programme, now in full operation following a pilot initiative, gives startups access to sovereign compute, technical support and training to support innovation across industries. Many of these companies are also members of the NVIDIA Inception program for cutting-edge startups — and as of today, access to some […]',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1659,'https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/podcast/called-to-serve-tech-research-and-positive-impact-with-chris-white/','https://microsoft.com/en-us/research/podcast/called-to-serve-tech-research-and-positive-impact-with-chris-white','Called to serve: Tech, research, and positive impact','calledtoservetechresearchandpositiveimpact','msr_blog','Microsoft Research blog','en','2026-10-20T16:00:00Z','2026-10-21T03:01:34Z','Lab Director Chris White has worked on research challenges with real-world implications—from new approaches to wartime data analysis to tools for combating human trafficking. He talks to program manager Weishung Liu about the influences that led to the work and more. The post Called to serve: Tech, research, and positive impact appeared first on Microsoft Research .',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1660,'https://eos.org/research-spotlights/using-machine-learning-techniques-for-polar-vortex-predictions','https://eos.org/research-spotlights/using-machine-learning-techniques-for-polar-vortex-predictions','Using Machine Learning Techniques for Polar Vortex Predictions','usingmachinelearningtechniquesforpolarvortexpredictions','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-20T13:30:09Z','2026-10-21T03:01:34Z','Disruptions to the polar vortex bring frigid temperatures to North America. A new model may show a way to better predict them. Credit: edward stojakovic/Flickr , CC BY 2.0',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1661,'https://eos.org/articles/white-dwarf-spotted-devouring-the-planet-it-birthed','https://eos.org/articles/white-dwarf-spotted-devouring-the-planet-it-birthed','White Dwarf Spotted Devouring the Planet It Birthed','whitedwarfspotteddevouringtheplanetitbirthed','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-20T13:29:54Z','2026-10-21T03:01:34Z','This artist’s concept shows the hypothetical second-generation planet in orbit around the white dwarf HS 0209+0832. The white dwarf produces enough ultraviolet radiation to strip the planet’s atmosphere away, creating a comet-like tail. Credit: NASA, ESA, L. Hustak (STScI)',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1662,'https://eos.org/thelandslideblog/the-aberfan-disaster-a-simple-guide-to-what-happened','https://eos.org/thelandslideblog/the-aberfan-disaster-a-simple-guide-to-what-happened','The Aberfan Disaster: a simple guide to what happened','theaberfandisasterasimpleguidetowhathappened','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-20T06:15:59Z','2026-10-21T03:01:34Z','To mark the 60th anniversary of the Aberfan disaster, I have reposted my (now unavailable) blog posts from a decade ago describing the tragedy. Tomorrow is the 60th anniversary of the terrible and tragic Aberfan landslide disaster in South Wales. Ten years ago I published a four part summary of what happened on that terrible […]',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1663,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-el-nio-impact-california-coastal.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-el-nio-impact-california-coastal.html','Where El Niño may impact California coastal areas this winter','whereelninomayimpactcaliforniacoastalareasthiswinter','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T00:20:12Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Driving across Highway 37 in the San Francisco Bay Area on the best of days already feels like driving on top of water. Buffeted on both sides by marshlands hosting egrets, herons, ducks and a handful of endangered species, the sea-level highway is a major connector linking Marin, Sonoma, Napa and Solano counties.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1664,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-forgotten-samples-greenland-cold-war.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-forgotten-samples-greenland-cold-war.html','Forgotten samples from Greenland''s Cold War base reveal warmer summers during ancient ice-free period','forgottensamplesfromgreenlandscoldwarbaserevealwarmersummersduringancienticefreeperiod','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T23:40:01Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','The site of a former U.S. military base in Greenland was ice-free at a time when summers were a few degrees warmer than today, according to a new University at Buffalo–led study of ancient sediment recovered from beneath the site in the 1960s.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1665,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wildfire-resilient-homes-residents.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wildfire-resilient-homes-residents.html','Building wildfire-resilient homes costs less than residents expect','buildingwildfireresilienthomescostslessthanresidentsexpect','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T23:20:02Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Housing development in Colorado is spreading into fire-prone landscapes, increasing wildfire risk and adding to the challenges of affordable housing and rising insurance costs.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1666,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-machine-reveals-potential-polar-vortex.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-machine-reveals-potential-polar-vortex.html','Machine learning reveals potential warning signal for polar vortex disruptions','machinelearningrevealspotentialwarningsignalforpolarvortexdisruptions','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T21:40:16Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','The polar vortex, a swirling mass of cold air encircling the North Pole, is sometimes disrupted by sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) events, unleashing waves of frigid air that penetrate farther south than usual. Temperatures across North America and Europe drop precipitously when this happens, but such events remain hard to predict, meaning we can''t easily warn people when SSW is imminent.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1667,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-tofu-winner-scientist-holistic-scoring.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-tofu-winner-scientist-holistic-scoring.html','Tofu is the winner. Scientist creates new holistic scoring system to measure the impact of food','tofuisthewinnerscientistcreatesnewholisticscoringsystemtomeasuretheimpactoffood','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T21:00:07Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Consumers are increasingly confronted with conflicting information surrounding how much protein they should have and which protein sources are healthiest, most sustainable, and best value for money. Cambridge research has uncovered the truth.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1668,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-seafloor-depths-fishing-free-software.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-seafloor-depths-fishing-free-software.html','From seafloor depths to fishing activity, free software brings ocean data together','fromseafloordepthstofishingactivityfreesoftwarebringsoceandatatogether','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T20:40:12Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','For scientists studying the ocean, finding the right data can sometimes be as challenging as figuring out what to do with it. Want to know seafloor depth, the location of underwater mountains or sea surface temperatures? The information exists, but it can take considerable time and computer coding to pull it together from different sources and get it into the proper format.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1669,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-california.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-california.html','What climate change is costing California','whatclimatechangeiscostingcalifornia','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T20:30:01Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','From heat-related illness and deaths to lost work hours and higher insurance and utility bills, climate change is already affecting California''s economy. A new statewide report in California''s Fifth Climate Change Assessment shows how the costs are rising, rippling across sectors and falling unevenly across the state—and in San Diego, the community is going to need to prepare for large-scale adaptation to much warmer conditions.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1670,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-cold-pulse-northwest-atlantic-reshaped.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-cold-pulse-northwest-atlantic-reshaped.html','How a cold pulse in the Northwest Atlantic reshaped East Coast fisheries','howacoldpulseinthenorthwestatlanticreshapedeastcoastfisheries','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T19:40:15Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Fishers in the Northeast began noticing an unusual shift in the summer of 2023. Some familiar catches, including shortfin and longfin squid, declined, while cold-water species such as mackerel bounced back from years of decline. A new study published in the Journal of Geophysical Research: Oceans finds that a pulse of cold, fresh water brought unusually cold conditions to the region, disrupting fisheries from New England to the Mid-Atlantic.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1671,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-bioeconomy-gaining-ground-worldwide.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-bioeconomy-gaining-ground-worldwide.html','The bioeconomy is gaining ground worldwide, report shows','thebioeconomyisgaininggroundworldwidereportshows','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T19:40:10Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Oil and natural gas still account for the vast majority of the world''s energy and raw materials, with all the associated environmental and biodiversity consequences. The bioeconomy is an approach to overcoming this dependence: Instead of using fossil fuels, it uses renewable resources such as plants, microorganisms and organic waste. The goal is for economic growth to go hand in hand with protecting species, the climate and soils.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1672,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-gaps-global-rainfall.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-gaps-global-rainfall.html','Filling the gaps in global rainfall maps','fillingthegapsinglobalrainfallmaps','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T19:30:01Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Global rainfall maps are built from observations collected by multiple satellites, but they have an important limitation. The satellites that provide these measurements cannot see the whole Earth at once. As a result, some regions of these maps can have no valid observations.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1673,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-limited-rural-communities-linked-high.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-limited-rural-communities-linked-high.html','Limited waste collection services in rural communities linked to high microplastic levels in Mekong River','limitedwastecollectionservicesinruralcommunitieslinkedtohighmicroplasticlevelsinmekongriver','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T19:10:11Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Plastics are accumulating in ecosystems around the world, and their tiny fragments can collect and carry chemical pollutants and pathogens into plants, animals and people. When fishermen in Cambodia''s Mekong River became alarmed about the amounts of plastic they were hauling up with each catch, they reached out to scientists for help.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1674,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-analysis-tool-coastal-communities-benefits.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-analysis-tool-coastal-communities-benefits.html','Cost-analysis tool allows coastal communities to see and estimate benefits of investing in storm-protection measures','costanalysistoolallowscoastalcommunitiestoseeandestimatebenefitsofinvestinginstormprotectionmeasures','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T18:40:15Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Today, as part of the official release of California''s Fifth Climate Change Assessment, researchers at the University of California, Santa Cruz, have launched a platform, data and tools that community leaders can use to estimate the block-by-block climate risks and the cost-effectiveness of adaptation solutions for reducing coastal damages.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1675,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-permitai-team-federal.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-permitai-team-federal.html','PermitAI team delivers a one-stop shop for federal permitting data','permitaiteamdeliversaonestopshopforfederalpermittingdata','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T18:20:25Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','The PermitAI team at the Department of Energy''s Pacific Northwest National Laboratory has changed how federal agencies search past environmental permitting decisions. Now it is taking that access one step further with two newly released publicly available resources that give instant access to both past decisions and live government data. Together, they cover the full federal permitting life cycle.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1676,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-central-coast-extremes-regional-california.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-central-coast-extremes-regional-california.html','Central Coast will face more extremes, regional report of California''s Fifth Climate Assessment finds','centralcoastwillfacemoreextremesregionalreportofcaliforniasfifthclimateassessmentfinds','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T18:00:19Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','California''s Central Coast is headed toward more climate extremes, with hotter days, stronger storms and more intense droughts.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1677,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-carbon-wood-stability-biochar-soil.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-carbon-wood-stability-biochar-soil.html','''Bomb carbon'' in wood shavings helped researchers measure the stability of biochar in soil','bombcarboninwoodshavingshelpedresearchersmeasurethestabilityofbiocharinsoil','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T17:20:30Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Biochar can store carbon in the soil and reduce the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere—yet we still know too little about how long the biochar will persist. Now, researchers from the Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences have found a new way to measure this, using wood from the 1960s that was affected by nuclear weapons testing. The paper is published in the journal Radiocarbon.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1678,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-large-animals-mitigate-climate-complicated.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-large-animals-mitigate-climate-complicated.html','Can restoring large animals help mitigate climate change? It''s complicated','canrestoringlargeanimalshelpmitigateclimatechangeitscomplicated','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T15:40:09Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Can protecting elephants and whales help mitigate climate change?',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1679,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ancient-wildfire-world.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ancient-wildfire-world.html','Ancient wildfire study offers warning for a warming world','ancientwildfirestudyofferswarningforawarmingworld','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T15:20:10Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','New research from the University of Aberdeen has revealed that periods of global warming can trigger long-lasting increases in wildfire activity, with ecosystems taking far longer to recover than previously thought. The study, recently published in Scientific Reports, provides the first detailed evidence of how wildfires responded to a major natural warming event around 65 million years ago, the Danian hyperthermal. This work offers important insights into the risks facing today''s world as climate change drives more frequent and severe fires.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1680,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-benefits-hospitals-landfills.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-benefits-hospitals-landfills.html','Study asks, Who benefits from where hospitals or landfills are located?','studyaskswhobenefitsfromwherehospitalsorlandfillsarelocated','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T14:40:25Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Cities are built around critical public facilities, some more desirable for neighbors than others. People want to live near hospitals and parks. Living near a wastewater plant or a landfill is another matter.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1681,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-river-reveals-ease-drought-unequal.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-river-reveals-ease-drought-unequal.html','River model reveals how coordinated water sharing could ease drought''s unequal effects','rivermodelrevealshowcoordinatedwatersharingcouldeasedroughtsunequaleffects','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T13:00:16Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','Rivers provide water for everything from growing crops and running factories to the taps in our homes, but droughts can leave them struggling to meet all these demands. A new study, published in Water Resources Research, suggests that changing how water is shared could help make limited supplies go further.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(1682,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-deep-sea-revisit-pacific-site.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-deep-sea-revisit-pacific-site.html','Deep-sea expedition will revisit Pacific mining site five and a half years after test','deepseaexpeditionwillrevisitpacificminingsitefiveandahalfyearsaftertest','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-20T09:40:02Z','2026-10-21T03:01:35Z','During Oct. 2026, the research vessel SONNE set sail from San Diego into the Pacific Ocean for expedition SO322. Its destination: the Clarion-Clipperton Zone north of the equator—the area that harbors the largest known deposits of polymetallic nodules on the seafloor. Germany, among others, holds an exploration license in this region.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
