INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2409,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-brazil-presidential-elections-linked-decades.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-brazil-presidential-elections-linked-decades.html','Brazil''s presidential elections linked to decades of fluctuating environmental destruction—study','brazilspresidentialelectionslinkedtodecadesoffluctuatingenvironmentaldestructionstudy','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T22:20:07Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','A University of Cambridge–led analysis of maps made from satellite imagery covering over three decades and seven presidents shows how the winner of Brazil''s national elections since 1990 directly affected the preservation of its globally vital ecosystems.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2410,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-farming-medieval-tsunamis-italian-lake.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-farming-medieval-tsunamis-italian-lake.html','How farming helped cause two medieval tsunamis in an Italian lake—and why the danger has returned','howfarminghelpedcausetwomedievaltsunamisinanitalianlakeandwhythedangerhasreturned','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T22:00:13Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','On Jan. 3, 1117, a strong earthquake shook northern Italy. A manuscript of the 12th century, kept today in the Vatican Library, recorded what came next: "The lakes also rose so much that very few of those living in the villages nearby escaped this danger."',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2411,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-microplastics-sediment-decades-human-worldwide.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-microplastics-sediment-decades-human-worldwide.html','Microplastics in sediment could trace decades of human activity worldwide','microplasticsinsedimentcouldtracedecadesofhumanactivityworldwide','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T20:20:19Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','In a review article recently published in the journal Earth-Science Reviews, researchers from the Leibniz Institute for Baltic Sea Research Warnemünde (IOW), in collaboration with colleagues from the University of Leicester (United Kingdom), report on how human activity has altered Earth''s sediments over the decades.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2412,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-highlights-policymakers-digital-policies-energy.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-highlights-policymakers-digital-policies-energy.html','Study highlights need for policymakers to look at digital policies as part of energy, climate planning','studyhighlightsneedforpolicymakerstolookatdigitalpoliciesaspartofenergyclimateplanning','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T20:00:14Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','A new study underscores the extent to which digital policies cannot be separated from energy policies. The research evaluates the relationship between information communication technologies and carbon dioxide emissions and finds that the variables the researchers used as proxies for digitalization were associated with increased emissions. The paper, "ICT and CO₂ Emissions in the G20: A Channel-Based Mediation Analysis," is published in the journal Sustainability.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2413,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-chiricahua-nation-national.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-chiricahua-nation-national.html','Chiricahua is the nation''s newest national park—what does that mean?','chiricahuaisthenationsnewestnationalparkwhatdoesthatmean','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T19:40:01Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','With a stroke of a pen, President Donald Trump has officially created the United States'' 64th national park.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2414,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-years-size-view-arctic-cloud.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-years-size-view-arctic-cloud.html','More than 20 years of data challenge one-size-fits-all view of Arctic cloud-forming particles','morethan20yearsofdatachallengeonesizefitsallviewofarcticcloudformingparticles','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T19:20:18Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','As the climate gets warmer, the number of particles that make up clouds in the Arctic may increase or decrease, depending on the specific area. A new study indicates that the Arctic should be divided into three parts rather than considered as a whole.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2415,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-entire-cities-extreme-reveals.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-entire-cities-extreme-reveals.html','We must prepare entire cities for extreme heat. Our new study reveals how','wemustprepareentirecitiesforextremeheatournewstudyrevealshow','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T19:20:02Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','In Australia, the days are growing longer and summer is fast approaching. But a very strong El Niño—a natural weather phenomenon that drives variations in temperature and winds across the Pacific—could make this summer a sweltering one.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2416,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-global-halted-emerging-ice-age.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-global-halted-emerging-ice-age.html','Global warming may have halted an emerging ice age, study suggests','globalwarmingmayhavehaltedanemergingiceagestudysuggests','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T18:00:10Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','Two thousand years ago, the Arctic remnants of a once-vast ice sheet started to grow again. It was the beginning of a new glacial period, more conversationally known as the next ice age—but by the 1960s, summer temperatures were already so high that the ice sheet started to shrink. In other words, global warming ended a period of glaciation that had already begun.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2417,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-earth-hemispheres-ice-ages.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-earth-hemispheres-ice-ages.html','Earth''s hemispheres may have worked together to drive past ice ages','earthshemispheresmayhaveworkedtogethertodrivepasticeages','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T18:00:06Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','New research from the University of St Andrews has uncovered a previously unknown process that may have helped drive Earth into past ice ages. Published today (Oct. 22) in the Journal of Climate, the paper links ice sheets in the Northern Hemisphere to changes in ocean circulation and sea ice in the Southern Hemisphere understood to drive down atmospheric CO₂. Linking these two key features of the ice ages has long been a challenge, given how far apart the two regions are.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2418,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-visuals-people-overlooked.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-visuals-people-overlooked.html','When climate data is transformed into visuals, some people and places get overlooked','whenclimatedataistransformedintovisualssomepeopleandplacesgetoverlooked','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T17:20:01Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','When a climate disaster happens, people thousands of miles away may encounter it through numbers: how many people died, how many were displaced, how many homes were destroyed and how much infrastructure was damaged. But for the people living through it, the disaster is not a data set. It is a home, a family, a livelihood and a place.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2419,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wildfires-amazon-emit-billion-metric.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wildfires-amazon-emit-billion-metric.html','Wildfires cause Amazon to emit 1.17 billion more metric tons of CO₂ than it absorbs','wildfirescauseamazontoemit117billionmoremetrictonsofco2thanitabsorbs','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T17:00:20Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','From 2010 to 2024, the Amazon rainforest—the largest tropical rainforest on the planet—released an average of 1.17 billion metric tons more carbon dioxide (CO₂) into the atmosphere than it absorbed each year. Wildfires were the main source, emitting approximately 1.47 billion metric tons of CO₂ per year—far exceeding the forest''s absorption capacity of 300 million metric tons per year.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2420,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-world-basic-income-trial-boosts.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-world-basic-income-trial-boosts.html','World-first basic income trial boosts Indigenous livelihoods and Amazon rainforest protection','worldfirstbasicincometrialboostsindigenouslivelihoodsandamazonrainforestprotection','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T15:30:07Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','A pilot project in which Indigenous people in the Peruvian Amazon were given an unconditional basic income significantly improved community well-being, strengthened food security and supported rainforest protection, according to analysis by the University of Bath released today.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2421,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-scientists-dangerous-oscillations-lake-varna.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-scientists-dangerous-oscillations-lake-varna.html','Scientists discover dangerous water oscillations between Lake Varna and Black Sea','scientistsdiscoverdangerouswateroscillationsbetweenlakevarnaandblacksea','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T11:00:10Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','For years, pilots have observed strong and unusual currents in the 2.4-kilometer-long (1.5-mile-long), 300-meter-wide (984-foot-wide) canal connecting Lake Varna with the Black Sea. Maarja Kruusmaa, an academician and professor at Tallinn University of Technology, and researcher Laura Piho, working with the Port of Varna, undertook detailed measurements to determine the causes of the phenomenon.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2422,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-india-capital-air-pollution-winter.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-india-capital-air-pollution-winter.html','India capital steps up air pollution controls ahead of winter','indiacapitalstepsupairpollutioncontrolsaheadofwinter','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T09:10:02Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','India began annual measures on Thursday to tackle toxic air pollution in New Delhi, a megacity of 30 million people regularly ranked among the world''s most polluted capitals.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2423,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-big-world-ground-climate.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-big-world-ground-climate.html','US is a big reason for the world losing ground in the fight against climate change, UN report says','usisabigreasonfortheworldlosinggroundinthefightagainstclimatechangeunreportsays','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T08:50:01Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','Losing more ground in the fight against climate change, humanity is heading for a hotter world than experts projected last year—and the United States is a big reason for that, a new United Nations report says.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2424,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-taiwan-issues-typhoon-champi-nears.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-taiwan-issues-typhoon-champi-nears.html','Taiwan issues warnings as Typhoon Champi nears','taiwanissueswarningsastyphoonchampinears','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T08:38:06Z','2026-10-23T03:01:00Z','A powerful typhoon headed toward Taiwan on Thursday, triggering the cancellation of dozens of flights and prompting warnings for locals to prepare for heavy rain, strong winds and giant waves.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
