INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3695,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/open-source-ai-tools-cbrain-dementia/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/open-source-ai-tools-cbrain-dementia','How AI Is Changing the Pace of Discovery for Millions Living With Dementia','howaiischangingthepaceofdiscoveryformillionslivingwithdementia','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-27T15:00:20Z','2026-10-28T04:01:28Z','There are 57 million people worldwide living with dementia today. For the researchers working to help them, the obstacle isn’t a lack of effort — it’s too much data, too few experts to synthesize it and a pace of discovery that can’t keep up with the disease. The Consortium for Biomedical Research and AI in […]',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3696,'https://eos.org/articles/twin-spacecraft-ready-to-head-for-a-martian-escapade','https://eos.org/articles/twin-spacecraft-ready-to-head-for-a-martian-escapade','Twin Spacecraft Ready to Head for a Martian Escapade','twinspacecraftreadytoheadforamartianescapade','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-27T13:17:07Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','ESCAPADE (Escape and Plasma Acceleration and Dynamics Explorers) launches from Cape Canaveral, in Florida, on 13 November 2025, after a 2-day delay caused by a solar storm. Credit: Blue Origin',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3697,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-food-power-strong-el-nio.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-food-power-strong-el-nio.html','Report indicates water, food and power at risk by strong El Niño','reportindicateswaterfoodandpoweratriskbystrongelnino','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-28T00:40:01Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','Rainfall reductions of up to 80% and torrential precipitation leading to severe flooding and landslides are the expected consequences across different areas of Latin America and the Caribbean from the impact of the El Niño phenomenon in 2027, according to a report co-authored by the General Secretariat of the Organization of American States (OAS), which urges member states to strengthen prevention and preparedness during the final months of 2026.',3.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3698,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-health-outpace-action-prompting.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-health-outpace-action-prompting.html','Climate health harms outpace action, prompting calls for global health emergency','climatehealthharmsoutpaceactionpromptingcallsforglobalhealthemergency','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-28T00:10:02Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','The 2026 Report of The Lancet Countdown on Health and Climate Change finds that many of the health risks and harms of climate change grew faster in the decade since the Paris Agreement was introduced (2016–2025) than in the decade before it (2006–2015).',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3699,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-earth-thermostat-pangea-era.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-earth-thermostat-pangea-era.html','The way Earth''s ''thermostat'' works may have changed during Pangea era','thewayearthsthermostatworksmayhavechangedduringpangeaera','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-27T23:20:06Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','Life arose on Earth at least 3.5 billion years ago, and our planet has remained habitable ever since—not too hot, not too cold. These life-sustaining conditions are possible in part thanks to a built-in, carbon cycle–based climate "thermostat" that controls the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3700,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-database-tracks-earth-standardizing-decades.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-database-tracks-earth-standardizing-decades.html','Open database tracks Earth''s water cycle by standardizing decades of satellite data','opendatabasetracksearthswatercyclebystandardizingdecadesofsatellitedata','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-27T22:20:13Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','All water comes from somewhere. Researchers who want to know where it comes from rely on water isotopes, variants of water molecules with slightly different masses, as location identifiers. In particular, the specific ratio of heavy to light water isotopes is unique to the water''s origin and processes it has undergone and can provide information on how water travels across the globe.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3701,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-satellite-florida-earlier-toxic.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-satellite-florida-earlier-toxic.html','AI and satellite data could give Florida earlier warnings of toxic red tides','aiandsatellitedatacouldgivefloridaearlierwarningsoftoxicredtides','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-27T21:50:01Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','Red tide, a harmful algal bloom that produces toxins, is known for causing respiratory irritation in humans and killing fish in coastal waters. Recent research shows that the conversation is also shifting to include a more subtle neurological threat.',2.0,'classified',NULL,36);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3702,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-urban-infrastructure-slowly-population-global.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-urban-infrastructure-slowly-population-global.html','Urban infrastructure grows more slowly than population, global analysis finds','urbaninfrastructuregrowsmoreslowlythanpopulationglobalanalysisfinds','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-27T20:40:10Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','As cities grow in population, they grow proportionally in terms of infrastructure—new buildings, paved streets and parking lots. Or so says conventional wisdom. However, a new study by a team of New York University researchers dispels this thinking.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3703,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-antarctic-reveal-chapter-glacier-history.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-antarctic-reveal-chapter-glacier-history.html','Antarctic rocks reveal a missing chapter of glacier history','antarcticrocksrevealamissingchapterofglacierhistory','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-27T20:30:05Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','Antarctica''s glaciers were changing long before satellites, weather stations or cameras captured their transformations, and they left behind clues. Along Marguerite Bay on the western Antarctic Peninsula, ridges of rocks and debris mark former glacier margins. For decades, scientists lacked direct evidence about when many of these ridges formed.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(3704,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-greenhouse-gases-highs.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-greenhouse-gases-highs.html','Greenhouse gases hit record highs, yet again','greenhousegaseshitrecordhighsyetagain','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-27T16:40:04Z','2026-10-28T04:01:30Z','Levels of carbon dioxide (CO₂) and other major greenhouse gases in the atmosphere reached unprecedented highs in 2025, locking in more long-term warming for the planet, according to a new report from the World Meteorological Organization (WMO).',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
