INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4581,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/ai-joins-the-search-for-extraterrestrial-signals/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/ai-joins-the-search-for-extraterrestrial-signals','Things That Go Beep in the Night: Agentic AI Joins the Search for Extraterrestrial Signals','thingsthatgobeepinthenightagenticaijoinsthesearchforextraterrestrialsignals','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-30T15:00:10Z','2026-10-31T04:03:31Z','While science fiction lore tells us that humans will meet extraterrestrial life in the form of little green men, the detail that should raise hairs this Halloween is this: the search for extraterrestrial intelligence took a major leap forward with AI, and it increasingly focuses on AI agents built by humans listening for AI agents […]',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4582,'https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/from-synthetic-enterprise-data-evaluations-to-real-microsoft-copilot-ai-confidence/','https://microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/from-synthetic-enterprise-data-evaluations-to-real-microsoft-copilot-ai-confidence','From synthetic enterprise data evaluations to real Microsoft Copilot AI confidence','fromsyntheticenterprisedataevaluationstorealmicrosoftcopilotaiconfidence','msr_blog','Microsoft Research blog','en','2026-10-30T13:05:28Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','Evaluating Microsoft Copilot at enterprise scale requires test data that captures the full complexity of real organizations, yet real enterprise data contains sensitive information that cannot be used for evaluation under any circumstances. The post From synthetic enterprise data evaluations to real Microsoft Copilot AI confidence appeared first on Microsoft Research .',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4583,'https://eos.org/editor-highlights/connecting-radio-wave-disruption-to-hot-charged-particles-from-afar','https://eos.org/editor-highlights/connecting-radio-wave-disruption-to-hot-charged-particles-from-afar','Connecting Radio Wave Disruption to “Hot” Charged Particles from Afar','connectingradiowavedisruptiontohotchargedparticlesfromafar','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-30T13:17:32Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','Schematics of a “hot flow anomaly” (HFA) accompanied by high-speed jets (HSJs) forming near the bow shock (BS) and generating transient disturbances in the magneto-sheath (MS; the region between the bow shock and the outer boundary of the magnetosphere, which is called the magnetopause.) These disturbances compress and distort the magnetopause (MP) and drive two localized convection vortices (circular arrows) in the “magnetic cusp” in the polar ionosphere. Field-aligned currents (FACs) are shown as blue magnetic field lines with arrows and connect the disturbed dayside region to the cusp ionos',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4584,'https://eos.org/articles/u-s-river-sediment-transport-is-becoming-burstier','https://eos.org/articles/u-s-river-sediment-transport-is-becoming-burstier','U.S. River Sediment Transport Is Becoming “Burstier”','usriversedimenttransportisbecomingburstier','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-30T13:02:34Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','Summer flash floods can introduce large amounts of sediment to the Colorado River. New research examines the rate of sediment transport in 175 rivers across the United States. Credit: Ryan Crow, USGS',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4585,'https://eos.org/research-spotlights/a-decade-of-carbon-dioxide-data-from-space-gets-an-upgrade','https://eos.org/research-spotlights/a-decade-of-carbon-dioxide-data-from-space-gets-an-upgrade','A Decade of Carbon Dioxide Data from Space Gets an Upgrade','adecadeofcarbondioxidedatafromspacegetsanupgrade','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-30T13:01:03Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','This image is a visual representation of atmospheric carbon dioxide levels as modeled by NASA’s Goddard Earth Observing System, which incorporates data from the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 satellite. Credit: Scientific Visualization Studio, NASA',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4586,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-hidden-erosion-reveals-soft-mountain.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-hidden-erosion-reveals-soft-mountain.html','Hidden erosion: Study reveals soft mountain rock erodes three times faster than hard rock—with climate implications','hiddenerosionstudyrevealssoftmountainrockerodesthreetimesfasterthanhardrockwithclimateimplications','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-30T20:40:01Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','A University of Massachusetts Amherst-led research team has discovered that weaker rock in mountain landscapes erodes three times faster than harder rock—even on gentler slopes—and is one of the main attributes governing mountain erosion. The finding also challenges our current understanding of the relationship between erosion and the release of carbon dioxide, and suggests that geologists and atmospheric scientists may be underestimating the CO₂ contributions coming from the Earth''s rocky places.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4587,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-decade-carbon-dioxide-space.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-decade-carbon-dioxide-space.html','A decade of carbon dioxide data from space gets an upgrade','adecadeofcarbondioxidedatafromspacegetsanupgrade','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-30T18:40:08Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','Every day, two NASA space instruments known as the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 and -3 (OCO-2 and OCO-3) capture more than a million observations of sunlight reflecting off Earth''s surface. Using the physics of how light travels through the air, an algorithm converts these raw data into atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO₂) concentrations.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4588,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-qa-deep-learningenhanced-air-quality.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-qa-deep-learningenhanced-air-quality.html','Q&A: Deep learning–enhanced air quality monitoring achieved using satellite data','qadeeplearningenhancedairqualitymonitoringachievedusingsatellitedata','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-30T18:00:09Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','A doctoral dissertation by Andrea Porcheddu, MSc in the field of Technical Physics will be examined at the Faculty of Science, Forestry and Technology, Kuopio campus.',2.0,'classified',NULL,44);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4589,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-discovery-bromochloropyrene-reveals-unexpected-biological.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-discovery-bromochloropyrene-reveals-unexpected-biological.html','Discovery of bromochloropyrene reveals unexpected biological properties','discoveryofbromochloropyrenerevealsunexpectedbiologicalproperties','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-30T16:00:04Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','Environmental pollutants containing chlorine or bromine have been widely studied, but compounds containing both elements have remained largely unexplored. Researchers have now detected bromochloropyrene in industrial soils and harbor sediments, investigated its breakdown under simulated sunlight, and measured its activity through a biological receptor linked to toxic effects. The findings reveal distinct environmental and biological properties, highlighting the need to investigate other mixed-halogen pollutants and improve future environmental monitoring and risk assessment.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4590,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-snowfall-clouds-east-antarctic-ice.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-snowfall-clouds-east-antarctic-ice.html','Snowfall and clouds may slow East Antarctic ice loss in a warmer world','snowfallandcloudsmaysloweastantarcticicelossinawarmerworld','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-30T14:40:08Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','A new modeling study, published in the journal Nature Climate Change, suggests that the East Antarctic Ice Sheet may be more resilient to extreme long-term warming than previously estimated. Increased snowfall, greater cloud cover and changes in atmospheric circulation could partially offset ice loss—even under very high carbon dioxide concentrations.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4591,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-pollution-people-indonesia-september.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-pollution-people-indonesia-september.html','Fire pollution killed 3,560 people in Indonesia in September: Report','firepollutionkilled3560peopleinindonesiainseptemberreport','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-30T09:30:05Z','2026-10-31T04:03:32Z','Pollution from smoke emitted by months of wildfires across Indonesia caused an estimated 3,560 deaths in the vast Southeast Asian archipelago in September, according to a new study.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
