INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5158,'https://eos.org/features/building-the-cities-of-tomorrow-with-living-materials','https://eos.org/features/building-the-cities-of-tomorrow-with-living-materials','Building the Cities of Tomorrow with Living Materials','buildingthecitiesoftomorrowwithlivingmaterials','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-11-02T13:09:43Z','2026-11-03T04:04:56Z','Incorporating engineered living materials (ELMs) into traditional concrete may offer increased sustainability and additional functionalities, such as self-healing properties. These ELM concrete slabs are at the OME, an experimental space at Newcastle University in the United Kingdom. Credit: Ben Bridgens, Hub for Biotechnology in the Built Environment',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5159,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-lesser-climate-impacts-global-reforestation.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-lesser-climate-impacts-global-reforestation.html','Study dives into lesser-known climate impacts of global reforestation','studydivesintolesserknownclimateimpactsofglobalreforestation','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-03T02:40:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Simon Fraser University researchers are zeroing in on how global forestation efforts impact our climate system beyond CO₂ removal in a new study.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5160,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-climate-tropics-hotter.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-climate-tropics-hotter.html','Climate change is making the tropics hotter','climatechangeismakingthetropicshotter','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-03T02:00:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','A new study finds that tropical areas often thought to be safe from the effects of climate change are actually experiencing significant changes in rainfall and extreme temperature events. The paper, "Shifting climate in Caribbean tropical forests: Long-term trends at La Selva (Costa Rica) and El Verde (Puerto Rico)," is published in PLOS Climate.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5161,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-uk-storm-surges-flung-global.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-uk-storm-surges-flung-global.html','UK storm surges have far-flung, global climate connections','ukstormsurgeshavefarflungglobalclimateconnections','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-03T01:20:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Where a storm surge strikes along a stretch of U.K. coastline is decided by global climate patterns in the Atlantic, Arctic and even further afield—according to new research led by Cambridge University.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5162,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-microbes-earth-antarctica-hidden-biodiversity.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-microbes-earth-antarctica-hidden-biodiversity.html','Microbes at the end of the Earth: Antarctica''s hidden biodiversity','microbesattheendoftheearthantarcticashiddenbiodiversity','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-03T00:20:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','When we think about Antarctica, many people imagine penguins, seals and whales. However, the majority of Antarctica''s scientific wonders cannot be seen without a microscope. These microbial communities form the foundation of Antarctic ecosystems yet remain largely invisible to most visitors.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5163,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-captures-northeast-winter-runoff-accurately.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-captures-northeast-winter-runoff-accurately.html','Improved snow model captures Northeast''s winter thaws and runoff more accurately','improvedsnowmodelcapturesnortheastswinterthawsandrunoffmoreaccurately','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-03T00:00:04Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Mountain snow in the Northeast is different. The region''s milder, more humid mountain climate shapes winter conditions that are different from those of the western United States. Yet, snow models don''t always capture those differences.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5164,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-arctic-roots-play-key-role.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-arctic-roots-play-key-role.html','Arctic plant roots may play key role in climate change','arcticplantrootsmayplaykeyroleinclimatechange','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T23:20:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','A new review involving researchers at Umeå University has revealed the critical role that plant roots play in shaping how Arctic tundra ecosystems respond to climate change. The paper, recently published in Nature Reviews Earth & Environment, highlights how processes occurring beneath the soil surface could help scientists better understand how one of the fastest-changing ecosystems on Earth will influence the future climate.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5165,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-fema-zone-home-neighbor.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-fema-zone-home-neighbor.html','Not in a FEMA flood zone? Your home could still be at risk—and you could suffer more than your neighbor','notinafemafloodzoneyourhomecouldstillbeatriskandyoucouldsuffermorethanyourneighbor','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T21:40:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','If your home isn''t in a federally designated flood zone, flooding might seem like someone else''s problem. No one requires you to buy flood insurance, and the map suggests you''re in the clear. But reality may be very different.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5166,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-reservoir-human-impact-ravi-river.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-reservoir-human-impact-ravi-river.html','Reservoir, human activity impact Ravi River''s surface water availability by fivefold','reservoirhumanactivityimpactraviriverssurfacewateravailabilitybyfivefold','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T21:00:21Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','What does a healthy river look like? For most of us, an obvious answer would involve two aspects: water flowing through a channel and green vegetation along its banks. However, in reality, a river''s health depends on several factors, such as how much water flows through it, how water moves through the landscape, how that flow changes with the seasons, how sediment moves downstream, how often it spills into its floodplain and how these processes change with the seasons.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5167,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-historical-reveal-bitter-truth-17th.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-historical-reveal-bitter-truth-17th.html','Historical records reveal the bitter truth about 17th-century Transylvania''s climate','historicalrecordsrevealthebittertruthabout17thcenturytransylvaniasclimate','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T20:17:08Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','For roughly five centuries from about 1300, Europe experienced periods of unusually cold weather and extreme conditions collectively known as the Little Ice Age. Transylvania, a historical region in modern-day Romania, was also affected, but information about just how severe things were had been scattered in old town records, church logs, travelogs and administrative and monastic diaries.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5168,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-term-marine-clouds-reveal-cloud.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-term-marine-clouds-reveal-cloud.html','Long-term observations of marine clouds reveal how in-cloud motions shape rain','longtermobservationsofmarinecloudsrevealhowincloudmotionsshaperain','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T19:40:15Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Research led by atmospheric scientists at the U.S. Department of Energy''s (DOE) Brookhaven National Laboratory sheds new light on one of the fundamental challenges of weather forecasting: determining the conditions under which a cloud will produce rain—and how much rain it will produce.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5169,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-large-scale-earth-ice-climate.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-large-scale-earth-ice-climate.html','UN reports warn of large-scale damage to Earth''s ice from climate change','unreportswarnoflargescaledamagetoearthsicefromclimatechange','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T18:54:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Exceeding 1.5C of global warming will have "irreversible" consequences for glaciers, sea ice and the Earth''s frozen regions, two U.N. reports warned on Monday, calling for urgent action.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5170,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-volcanic-eruptions-tropics-major-factor.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-volcanic-eruptions-tropics-major-factor.html','Volcanic eruptions in the tropics are a major factor in Earth''s thermostat','volcaniceruptionsinthetropicsareamajorfactorinearthsthermostat','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T17:40:06Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Volcanic eruptions are known for their spectacular visual displays of vivid lava, as well as the devastating impacts they can have on the environment and the communities who live nearby. But they can also have far-reaching impacts across the globe.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5171,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-climate-vulnerable-africa-significant-gaps.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-climate-vulnerable-africa-significant-gaps.html','Climate-vulnerable Africa faces significant gaps in early warning systems, leaving millions at risk from extreme weather','climatevulnerableafricafacessignificantgapsinearlywarningsystemsleavingmillionsatriskfromextremeweather','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T16:40:20Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Climate-related events affected at least 13 million people across Africa in 2025. Over the last decade alone, extreme weather has affected populations in Africa disproportionately. Tropical Cyclone Freddy (February 2023) caused widespread flooding and led to over 1,500 deaths, and in 2015–16 a drought in southern Africa led to widespread crop failure and food insecurity for 40 million people.',6.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5172,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-youre-unclean-air.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-youre-unclean-air.html','Worried you''re breathing unclean air? Here''s how to measure it','worriedyourebreathinguncleanairhereshowtomeasureit','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T16:00:08Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','A breath of fresh air does wonders for the body and mind.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5173,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-community-urban.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-community-urban.html','Community input strengthens urban flood adaptation model','communityinputstrengthensurbanfloodadaptationmodel','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T14:40:06Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Urban flooding causes significant social, environmental and economic losses every year. In Baltimore, those losses have increased in frequency and intensity since flooding records began in 1748 and are expected to continue doing so as the climate changes. Traditional flood prevention approaches, which use concrete and other impervious surfaces to direct flow, often fail in urban settings because they impede rainwater infiltration.',3.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5174,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-national-states-steepest-trends.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-national-states-steepest-trends.html','National study pinpoints states with steepest warming trends since 1980','nationalstudypinpointsstateswithsteepestwarmingtrendssince1980','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-02T14:20:01Z','2026-11-03T04:04:57Z','Global warming, the observed increase in Earth''s average global surface temperature since preindustrial times (1850–1900), currently stands at around 1.63 degrees Celsius. While this number is expected to continue to rise, local temperatures will not go up uniformly around the world. In regions where warming trends are most pronounced, local adaptation and resilience measures will be especially critical to minimizing risks to ecosystems, agricultural productivity, the built environment, human health and economic well-being.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
