INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5968,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-explores-offshore-marine-food-webs.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-explores-offshore-marine-food-webs.html','New research explores how offshore wind development may alter marine food webs','newresearchexploreshowoffshorewinddevelopmentmayaltermarinefoodwebs','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T23:00:01Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','As offshore wind development expands along the U.S. Atlantic Coast, understanding its potential effects on marine ecosystems is increasingly important.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5969,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ancient-maya-mercury-severe-environmental.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ancient-maya-mercury-severe-environmental.html','Ancient Maya mercury use may have come at a severe environmental cost','ancientmayamercuryusemayhavecomeatasevereenvironmentalcost','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T19:20:08Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','Ancient Maya communities made extensive use of liquid mercury and a red, mercury-containing pigment called cinnabar to decorate buildings, pottery and burial sites. Unsurprisingly, as previous research has shown, this led to mercury contamination of soils and reservoirs at some Maya sites. And that pollution may have spread even farther into their territory and caused the collapse of two lake ecosystems, according to a study published in the journal Science Advances.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5970,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ice-age-abrupt-climate-shifts.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ice-age-abrupt-climate-shifts.html','Ice-age abrupt climate shifts were not as fast as thought, new study finds','iceageabruptclimateshiftswerenotasfastasthoughtnewstudyfinds','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T19:00:34Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','New high-resolution speleothem records from China and India, together with isotope-enabled climate models, show that abrupt glacial ocean circulation recoveries took centuries, not decades—a finding with important implications for how we assess future Atlantic circulation change. The work is published in the journal Science.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5971,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-explore-ai-earthquake-safety.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-explore-ai-earthquake-safety.html','Researchers explore how AI could improve earthquake safety','researchersexplorehowaicouldimproveearthquakesafety','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T18:20:05Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','Researchers at the University of East London (UEL) have developed a new framework for assessing earthquake risks, using AI-assisted technology to examine how soil conditions, ground shaking and structural strength influence a building''s ability to withstand seismic activity.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5972,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-european-database-environmental-human-pharmaceuticals.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-european-database-environmental-human-pharmaceuticals.html','New European database to support environmental risk assessment of human pharmaceuticals','neweuropeandatabasetosupportenvironmentalriskassessmentofhumanpharmaceuticals','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T17:40:12Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','The PREMIER project, a public-private European consortium of research organizations (like Radboud University) and international pharmaceutical companies, has launched a new database to support the environmental risk assessment (ERA) of human pharmaceuticals. The data is publicly accessible, free of charge. The database was built with key user requirements in mind.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5973,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-climate-undermining-health-burden-falls.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-climate-undermining-health-burden-falls.html','Climate change is undermining our health, and the burden falls heaviest on women','climatechangeisunderminingourhealthandtheburdenfallsheaviestonwomen','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T16:00:09Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','As sea levels creep higher in the Pacific, salt leaches into the soil and reduces the land available to grow food. Ocean warming is damaging coral reefs, reducing fish stocks, and salinity has tainted freshwater supplies.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5974,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-doctors-teachers-lawyers-duty-align.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-doctors-teachers-lawyers-duty-align.html','From doctors and teachers to lawyers, every profession has a duty to align with climate goals','fromdoctorsandteacherstolawyerseveryprofessionhasadutytoalignwithclimategoals','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T15:00:15Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','A Dutch family doctor named Tibor Poelmann posted a petition on an online forum for general practitioners (GPs) in 2021. He wanted their pension fund to stop investing in oil and gas. It quickly gathered 1,200 signatures.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5975,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-large-pedestrian-crowds-distinctive-seismic.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-large-pedestrian-crowds-distinctive-seismic.html','Coordinated activity gives large pedestrian crowds a distinctive seismic signature','coordinatedactivitygiveslargepedestriancrowdsadistinctiveseismicsignature','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T15:00:12Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','Large pedestrian crowds generate distinctive seismic signals depending on their collective behavior, according to a new study that includes data from Tokyo schools and outdoor gatherings in the United States and Israel. These seismic signals are strongest in the 1–2 hertz frequency band, which is consistent with human walking frequencies.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5976,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-australia-seabed-site-japanese-company.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-australia-seabed-site-japanese-company.html','Australia approves seabed site where a Japanese company will store carbon pollution','australiaapprovesseabedsitewhereajapanesecompanywillstorecarbonpollution','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-05T08:56:32Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','Australia has designated a seabed storage site near its northern coast where a Japanese company plans to store massive amounts of carbon pollution using a technology that has divided climate scientists.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(5977,'https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1028454/openai-data-center-nda','https://theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1028454/openai-data-center-nda','OpenAI is stepping back from NDAs for data center projects','openaiissteppingbackfromndasfordatacenterprojects','theverge_ai','The Verge - AI','en','2026-11-06T00:37:56Z','2026-11-06T04:01:46Z','Following this week''s midterms where voters resoundingly voiced their opposition to data center expansion, OpenAI today announced new commitments to communities where it is working on data center projects, including that it won''t use non-disclosure agreements (NDAs) with public officials. Many data center projects have come under scrutiny for relying on NDAs to hide discussions […]',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
