INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6880,'https://eos.org/research-spotlights/one-shock-many-responses-in-zircon-and-other-grains','https://eos.org/research-spotlights/one-shock-many-responses-in-zircon-and-other-grains','One Shock, Many Responses in Zircon and Other Grains','oneshockmanyresponsesinzirconandothergrains','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-11-09T14:23:25Z','2026-11-10T04:04:58Z','A zircon grain shocked at 40 gigapascals (equivalent to a lower mantle pressure in Earth) partially converts to zones of its high-pressure form, reidite. The image was produced using electron backscatter diffraction analysis. Credit: Kim A. Cone, University of Rochester',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6881,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-fossil-emissions-decrease-energy-growth.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-fossil-emissions-decrease-energy-growth.html','Fossil CO₂ emissions projected to decrease in 2026 amid clean energy growth and Iran conflict','fossilco2emissionsprojectedtodecreasein2026amidcleanenergygrowthandiranconflict','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-10T01:00:05Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','Global carbon emissions from fossil fuels are projected to decrease by 0.4% in 2026—driven by expansion of solar and wind power and the Iran conflict—according to new research by the Global Carbon Project. The 2026 edition (the 21st annual report) is published in the journal Earth System Science Data.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6882,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-el-nio-polycrisis-health-energy.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-el-nio-polycrisis-health-energy.html','El Niño 2026–27 as a polycrisis: What happens when health and energy systems fail simultaneously?','elnino202627asapolycrisiswhathappenswhenhealthandenergysystemsfailsimultaneously','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-10T00:00:11Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','Open any news feed from the past few months, and the disasters arrive in a stream: smoke reaching dangerous levels throughout Canada and flash floods in Ottawa, Montréal and Toronto; heat and drought arriving together on every continent.',3.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6883,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-tin-deposits-source-cooling.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-tin-deposits-source-cooling.html','Tin deposits are formed from source rocks, not cooling','tindepositsareformedfromsourcerocksnotcooling','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T23:20:19Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','New research from the University of St Andrews has uncovered the primary drivers of tin mineralization, which will have significant implications for the discovery of tin deposits critical to the energy transition.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6884,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-regions-15c-warmer-world.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-regions-15c-warmer-world.html','Some regions may already be living in a 1.5°C warmer world','someregionsmayalreadybelivingina15cwarmerworld','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T22:50:01Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','Regions worldwide may have already experienced around 90%–94% of the warming expected when the global average reaches 1.5°C above preindustrial levels, and 65%–76% of that expected at 2°C of warming. The findings, published in Nature Climate Change, suggest that regional warming in 2025 was statistically indistinguishable from the levels expected in a 1.5°C warmer world, underlining the immediate need for adaptation across all regions.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6885,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-science-reroutes-truckloads-excess-food.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-science-reroutes-truckloads-excess-food.html','Computer science researcher reroutes truckloads of excess food from landfills to food pantries','computerscienceresearcherreroutestruckloadsofexcessfoodfromlandfillstofoodpantries','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T22:20:01Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','The planet already grows enough food to feed everyone. The problem is one of logistics. A third of all the food grown and raised goes to waste, meaning that one in 12 people does not have enough to eat.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6886,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ai-tool-crisis-communication.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ai-tool-crisis-communication.html','Researchers develop AI tool to improve crisis communication','researchersdevelopaitooltoimprovecrisiscommunication','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T21:20:13Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','During a disaster, emergency managers need quick, reliable data to make decisions, but the vast and changing stream of information that emerges during a crisis can make it difficult to distinguish facts from noise.',2.0,'classified',NULL,62);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6887,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-southern-ocean-fuel-earth-warmest.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-southern-ocean-fuel-earth-warmest.html','How the Southern Ocean helped fuel Earth''s warmest climate in 65 million years','howthesouthernoceanhelpedfuelearthswarmestclimatein65millionyears','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T19:20:04Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','About 50 million years ago, Earth experienced its warmest climate since the extinction of the dinosaurs. Global temperatures were more than 10°C higher than today, and atmospheric CO₂ concentrations reached around 1,600 parts per million. But what kept CO₂ levels so extraordinarily high for millions of years? An international research team led by the Max Planck Institute for Chemistry has identified an important piece of the puzzle: the Southern Ocean.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6888,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-east-west-geological-crossroads-iran.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-east-west-geological-crossroads-iran.html','Where East meets West: Tracing the geological crossroads of Iran and Afghanistan','whereeastmeetswesttracingthegeologicalcrossroadsofiranandafghanistan','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T19:10:01Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','Iran and Afghanistan have a certain iconic status as largely inhospitable areas, rich in raw materials. But where does this remarkable geology come from? Researchers from the University of Central Asia and Utrecht University have now reconstructed the history of this ''geological Silk Road'' in detail. "Culturally, the region lies at the crossroads of European, Central Asian, East Asian, Indian and Arab influences. Our reconstruction shows that the same is true geologically." Their results are published in the journal Gondwana Research.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6889,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-chemical-fingerprints-reveal-ancient-pueblo.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-chemical-fingerprints-reveal-ancient-pueblo.html','Chemical fingerprints reveal ancient Pueblo blue mineral sources','chemicalfingerprintsrevealancientpueblobluemineralsources','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T19:00:33Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','Kari Schleher, an assistant professor in the University of New Mexico''s Department of Anthropology and faculty curator of archaeology at UNM''s Maxwell Museum, recently published research examining whether archaeologists can determine the sources of azurite, a vivid blue copper mineral that was used by the Ancestral Pueblo people starting at least 1,400 years ago.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6890,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-hottest-years.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-hottest-years.html','2026 set to be one of the 4 hottest years on record','2026settobeoneofthe4hottestyearsonrecord','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T18:00:25Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','The year 2026 continues the protracted streak of exceptional global temperatures, melting ice and rising sea levels, with a very strong El Niño event set to unleash even more heat and extreme weather well into 2027, according to a new report from the World Meteorological Organization (WMO). It highlights the knock-on risks to the environment, economy and everyday life.',3.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6891,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-intense-storms-climate-extreme-urban.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-intense-storms-climate-extreme-urban.html','Less rain, more intense storms: Climate change strengthens extreme urban floods','lessrainmoreintensestormsclimatechangestrengthensextremeurbanfloods','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T17:40:20Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','A new study reveals a troubling consequence of climate change: Even as rainfall decreases, cities could face increasingly severe flooding. The study was led by Ph.D. student Raz Nussbaum and co-authored by Dr. Moshe Armon and supervised by professor Efrat Morin from the Hebrew University of Jerusalem.',4.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6892,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-satellite-world-oceans-restricted-legal.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-satellite-world-oceans-restricted-legal.html','Satellite monitoring of the world''s oceans is being restricted by legal barriers, study suggests','satellitemonitoringoftheworldsoceansisbeingrestrictedbylegalbarriersstudysuggests','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T17:20:16Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','Satellite technology can increasingly track vessels across the world''s oceans and has the ability to detect industrial fishing inside marine protected areas (MPAs). But new research has suggested that regulations governing these often vulnerable parts of our planet could be preventing global authorities from turning satellite detection into effective enforcement.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6893,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-seismic-imaging-reveals-slab-subductioncollision.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-seismic-imaging-reveals-slab-subductioncollision.html','Seismic imaging reveals double slab tearing at a subduction–collision transition','seismicimagingrevealsdoubleslabtearingatasubductioncollisiontransition','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T16:20:04Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','An international research team has used seismic imaging to identify two slab tears beneath the Zagros–Makran transition zone located in southeastern Iran near the Strait of Hormuz. The findings suggest that the tears formed through distinct geodynamic mechanisms and may have influenced regional tectonic deformation and magmatic activity.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6894,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ancient-underwater-canyons-reveal-clues.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ancient-underwater-canyons-reveal-clues.html','Ancient underwater canyons reveal clues to the world''s oldest animal life','ancientunderwatercanyonsrevealcluestotheworldsoldestanimallife','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T15:00:06Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','Geologists have uncovered new evidence that dramatic sea-level changes carved giant underwater canyons in South Australia''s Flinders Ranges hundreds of millions of years ago, helping to create conditions that preserved some of the earliest complex life on Earth. The findings, led by Adelaide University, shed new light on the environmental changes that accompanied the rise of the mysterious Ediacaran biota.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6895,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-cop31-leaders-pressure-tackle-climate.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-cop31-leaders-pressure-tackle-climate.html','COP31 opens with leaders under pressure to tackle climate crisis','cop31openswithleadersunderpressuretotackleclimatecrisis','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T13:20:05Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','The COP31 climate talks kicked off Monday with an early fight over money as the U.N.''s chief urged world leaders to "get serious" about tackling the crisis after a year of devastating weather extremes.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6896,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-crystals-hint-volcano-ability-erupt.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-crystals-hint-volcano-ability-erupt.html','Crystals hint at a volcano''s ability to erupt','crystalshintatavolcanosabilitytoerupt','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T10:00:06Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','University of Queensland researchers have shown tiny crystals are a window to activity inside volcanoes and could provide clues to when they might erupt.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(6897,'https://phys.org/news/2026-11-life-world-dirtiest-skies.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-11-life-world-dirtiest-skies.html','''Burning'': Life under the world''s dirtiest skies','burninglifeundertheworldsdirtiestskies','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-11-09T09:40:01Z','2026-11-10T04:04:59Z','On the outskirts of India''s capital, a short motorbike ride through the urban area with the world''s worst air pollution leaves salesman Kanwar Singh coated in toxic dust.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
